  "articleBody": "Bathroom floor tile is built to last decades, but cutting corners during the prep or setting phases will quickly ruin an otherwise beautiful remodel. If you are preparing to hire a tile contractor, knowing the common pitfalls helps you spot quality workmanship.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n1. Tiling directly over bouncy subfloors: Mortar does not add structural strength. Without proper subfloor thickness or uncoupling underlayment, floor movement will snap tile and loosen grout lines.\n2. Skipping the dry layout: Setting tile straight out of the box without mapping layout lines often leaves ugly, one-inch slivers against the tub or vanity. A dry layout balances the floor visually.\n3. Using the wrong trowel size: Using a trowel with small notches leaves inadequate mortar coverage under large tiles. Floors require at least 85% to 95% mortar coverage to prevent cracked tiles from foot pressure.\n4. Forgetting to back-butter large-format tile: Tiles 15 inches or larger need mortar skimmed on their back side before setting to eliminate air pockets.\n5. Grouting or walking on fresh tile too soon: Thinset requires at least 24 hours to cure before receiving grout or regular foot traffic. Rushing this step breaks the adhesive bond.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nGetting an even, lippage-free floor that stands up to family traffic requires skilled hands and proper prep. To hire a dedicated team for your bathroom floor tile in the Denver metro area, call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
